#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""stupidsim-linux: sets up x86Sim's iOS simulator on Linux for stupidsim, fast enough for throwaway
machines (cloud agent sessions, CI), and records what an app needs from the iOS runtime.

    stupidsim-linux setup [--app NAME ...] [--device NAME] [--runtime RUNTIME] [--no-boot]
        Install x86Sim, fetch the runtime's base and the named apps' parts from the store, restore
        the translation cache and a booted-once device, boot it and wait for the home screen.
    stupidsim-linux boot [--device NAME] [--metal auto|gpu|software]
    stupidsim-linux shutdown [--device NAME]
    stupidsim-linux trace begin [--device NAME]
        Shut the device down, mark every runtime file unread, and boot it again. Then use the app.
    stupidsim-linux trace end NAME [--base base]
        Write manifests/NAME.txt: the runtime files read since `trace begin`, less those in --base.
    stupidsim-linux store build [--device NAME] [--manifest NAME ...]
        From a machine with the whole runtime: write the store's files for this runtime to
        $STUPIDSIM_STORE_DIR (skeleton, a pack per manifest, translation cache, device template).
    stupidsim-linux store publish
        Upload $STUPIDSIM_STORE_DIR to the store.
    stupidsim-linux store presign KEY [--hours N]
        A time-limited signed GET URL for one object (rarely needed now that reads are public).

The store is a Cloudflare R2 bucket. Setup reads it anonymously from its public address,
https://stupidsim-store.stupidtech.net ($STUPIDSIM_PUBLIC_STORE overrides it), so it needs no
credentials. Publishing writes through the bucket's S3 endpoint, $STUPIDSIM_STORE_URL
(https://<account>.r2.cloudflarestorage.com/<bucket>), with $STUPIDSIM_STORE_KEY_ID and
$STUPIDSIM_STORE_SECRET; curl signs those requests (--aws-sigv4). When the key is set, reads are
signed too. Layout, per runtime build:

    runtimes/<runtime>/skeleton.tar.zst      every directory and symlink, and the files outside
                                             RuntimeRoot (the simruntime's own plists)
    runtimes/<runtime>/packs/<name>.tar.zst  the files a manifest lists (base, then one per app)
    runtimes/<runtime>/manifests/<name>.txt  those lists
    runtimes/<runtime>/warm/jitcache.tar.zst x86Sim's translation cache for the runtime
    runtimes/<runtime>/warm/device.tar.zst   a device that has booted once on it
    runtimes/<runtime>/warm/device.json      that device's original path and name
    current.json                             {"runtime": ..., "x86sim": ...}

Only Python's standard library, curl, tar and zstd are used. Verified with x86Sim 0.3.0 and the iOS
27.2 runtime (24B8117) on Ubuntu 24.04.
"""
